From 5fdff1bf7a8c6a5d11b324b40b8023a49d8dbc54 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 16:07:42 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_ui, gpui: the floating thread controls scroll with a
 short ease-out
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zeo next §6.52, second step (operator, 2026-11-26). The floating controls' four jumps --
previous and next user message, top, end -- move the list over 200 ms with an ease-out, one
step per frame, instead of cutting to the target, and end on the same `scroll_to` (or
`scroll_to_end`) they used before: where the view lands is unchanged, only the way there is
seen.

gpui: ListState gains `pixel_offset_of(ListOffset)`, `scroll_top_pixel_offset()` and
`scroll_to_pixel_offset(Pixels)`, the "pixel offset of an item" the proposal said this needs.
They read and set the scroll top by the heights measured so far. `scroll_to_pixel_offset`
calls no scroll handler, so the thread's handler, which already runs on every wheel event,
tells the user's scrolling from the program's and ends the animation where it stands.

Why a jump starts one viewport short: the thread list measures only around the viewport
(2048 px of overdraw, no measure_all), so pixel offsets far away are guesses and sliding
through unmeasured rows would lay them out frame after frame. A jump longer than a viewport
therefore begins a viewport before its target, on the side it comes from, inside measured
rows; ending on `scroll_to` absorbs whatever the guesses were off by.

Where it does not animate: under reduce_motion; to the end while the agent writes (following
the tail is a scroll of its own, and two would fight); and every other `scroll_to` in the
thread -- sending, restoring a position, opening at a task -- which nobody asked to watch.
Dragging the scrollbar ends the animation too (the drag calls no handler, so `render` checks).

Tests: gpui list `test_pixel_offsets_and_scrolling_to_one` (offsets on 20 px rows, clamped at
the top, no handler call); agent_ui thread_view::scroll_animation::tests (start one viewport
short either way, short jumps start in place, reduce motion and a written tail do not animate,
frame steps move forward and land exactly on scroll_to, nothing to do when already there).
Not covered by a test: the wheel ending the animation inside a thread view.
